pc|HT|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The @H10 is the standard lead if you play ^*Bcoded tens^*n then the lead would be the 9. I am not suggesting you necessarily play coded tens, but let me explain the hows and whys.|
PG| ***** 23 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Many experts play the lead of the jack against NT denies anything higher which can be useful. But then what do you lead from AJ10 or KJ10 if you play that. |
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The standard lead is the jack. On the other hand, if you want to play ^*Bjack denies anything higher^*n then you have to play that you lead the 10 from AJ10, KJ10, and also 109. |
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That is called a "coded 10" lead. So playing coded, the lead of the 10 shows AJ10 KJ10 or nothing higher than the 10.|
PG| ***** 26 ***** |


