qx|A3|st||rh||ah|Board 3 IMPs   -    March 27, 2024|pn|hondo717,danpsych,IraCT,Sputniks|
md|1SKQJT8HAQ8D4CJ852,S952HJ74DA2C97643,S3HKT932DK953CAKQ,SA764H65DQJT876CT|sv|e|
mb|1S|mb|P|mb|2Hp|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|2@H over 1@S always promises a 5+ card suit. Based on that, since we have a fit, let me count |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=dummypoints,Dummy Points^*N|CP||FP||at|. My hand has 13 HCP, plus 1 quality suit point, 1 length point, and 2 points for singleton with 3 card support for 17 points. That is enough to at least try for slam.|
PG| ***** 47 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|An important part of 2/1 game forcing is the principle of ^*BFast Arrival^*n. 2@H by North establishes a game force. Therefore, if I bid anything below game partner must continue bidding.|
PG| ***** 48 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If I jump to 4@H, that is a weak bid, showing a minimal opener and little slam interest hence ^*BFast Arrival^*n. If I bid 3@H, that shows at least some slam interest. And if the partner is interested he can make a control bid.|
PG| ***** 49 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^^B@2^*BA Kib: ^*NWould 4@D be a splinter and also show slam interest?|
PG| ***** 50 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^^
^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Yes, 4@D would be a |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=splinters,Splinter^*N|CP||FP||at|. It is typically is done with 4-card support. Since I only have 3 hearts, I felt 3@H was the better call. As you will see, I can show my singleton diamond later in the auction.|
PG| ***** 51 ***** |


mb|3H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P?|mb|4C|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Normally in this position with a singleton North would bid 3@S to show 1st or 2nd round control in spades. But since I bid spades, North should only control bid with an ace or king in spades and not a singleton. Hence 4@C denies a spade honor and shows a club control.|
PG| ***** 52 ***** |


mb|P|mb|4D|at|^^
My 4@D bid shows a diamond control based on shortness.|
PG| ***** 53 ***** |


mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|4S|an|RKCB|
pg| ***** 54 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I like to play |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| meaning, when hearts are trump, 4@S is RKCB. The advantage of |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=kickback,Kickback^*N|CP||FP||at| has to do with the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at|.|
PG| ***** 55 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Say we played 4N as RKCB and I bid 5@D (1 or 4 keys). Then partner would like to be able to do the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at|, although cannot do so without going past 5@H.|
PG| ***** 56 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|By playing 4@S as RKCB, I would bid 5@C with 1 or 4 and 5@D is available as the |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at|. In this case, since we are missing two key cards, no need for |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=queenask,Queen Ask^*N|CP||FP||at|.|
PG| ***** 57 ***** |


mb|P|mb|5C|an|1 or 4|
